  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 411 PM EDT FRI JUL 31 2026\n\nThe long-term period dawns Sunday morning with a weak, closed ~575\ndam low spinning over southwest Indiana within a positively- \ntilted trough that extends northeastward toward James Bay, where a\nbroader 500 hPa trough is pivoting across eastern Canada. This \nupper-level low will lie nearly vertically stacked atop an \noccluding ~1004 mb surface low. While that does not sound all \nthat impressive, the NAEFS and ECMWF ENS show that MSLP values of \nthis depth fall below 1979-2009 CFSR climatology, indicative of \nhow rare it is to have a developed mid-latitude cyclone passing \nthrough the Ohio Valley this time of year. The system's cold \nfront will extend from the eastern side of the low near Cincinnati,\nsouthward along the I-75 corridor into Tennessee, and then \nsouthward into the Lower Mississippi Valley.\n\nWhile guidance still shows the northern stream 500 hPa trough\npivoting across Eastern Canada and adjacent portions of the  \nGreat Lakes and Northeast later Sunday and Sunday night and \nstealing some of the energy from the southern stream trough/ \nclosing low, the 31/12z model suite seems to be latching onto more\nof the vorticity energy remaining with the closed low and \ngyrating over the Ohio Valley and Central Appalachians on Monday \nand Tuesday. That system only slowly departs later in the week on \nWednesday and Thursday as broad upper-level troughing develops \nover the North Central to Northeast CONUS, and an associated belt\nof westerlies aloft captures the remnant low and sweeps it away. \nFlow over the Ohio Valley trends quasi-zonal by late week.\n\nAt the surface, the low pressure system over the lower Ohio Valley\nremains nearly vertically stacked as it drifts to over the Mid-\nOhio Valley on Monday, where it wobbles and largely dissipates \nbefore its remnants are pulled away to the north by mid-week. As a\nresult, the system's cold front will slowly cross the JKL CWA \nduring the day on Sunday. Within the warm sector, some of the \nlongest-range CAMs are hinting at the possibility of enough shear \nand instability for at least weakly organized convection ahead of \nthe cold front again on Sunday afternoon, especially closer to VA \nand WV. With the upper-level low lingering aloft, PWATs are now \nlikely to stay elevated through the day on Sunday, as well as \nMonday and Tuesday (more so than what was expected in recent \ndays). The result will be a lot more cloud cover and diurnally- \nmodulated convection with greatest rainfall coverage during the \nafternoon and evening and continued though lower rain chances at \nnight. The combination of a lingering nearly saturated deep warm \ncloud layer, skinny CAPE profiles, and elevated PWATs in the 1.75\nto 2.00 inch range, warranted the addition of Marginal Risk EROs \nfor both Monday and Tuesday. The threat for heavy rainfall \nsubsides later in the week as the upper-level low departs and \nincreasingly zonal flow allows drier and warmer air to filter \neastward from the Plains mid-week. While a few pop-up \nthunderstorms will be possible from Thursday through Saturday, \nactivity should remain more isolated to perhaps scattered and be \nconfined largely to the afternoon and evening hours.\n\nIn sensible weather terms, look for a relative lull in the rainfall\nSunday morning, giving way to more widespread shower and thunderstorm\nactivity during the afternoon. Some of the convection could be strong\nwith gusty winds and torrential downpours. It will be humid and mild\nwith highs mainly in the lower 80s. Rain chances will then follow a\ndiurnal cycle on Monday and Tuesday, with coverage greater during the\nafternoon and evening but still remaining possible at night. It will\nbe quite humid and muggy with afternoon highs mainly in the lower 80s\nand nighttime lows in the mid to upper 60s. The pattern turns drier\nfrom Wednesday onward with more sunshine and daily high temperatures\nwarming into the mid to perhaps upper 80s, while nighttime lows \nrange from 65 to 70F.",
